4.36.m:
The serpents, enemies of Garuḍa, took refuge in Śaṅkara;
out of compassion he wears upon his own limbs those very ones who sought refuge.
I am his mount in the form of a bull; no other is able to bear him;
formerly, at the slaying of Tripura, [the bull] arose from a part of a portion of me.
Having offered fragrant flowers such as the pārijāta, sandalwood paste, and the like
unto me, he has no attachment to them at all.
His delight is ever in dhattūra, in anointing with bilva leaves,
in the scentless and the fragrant, and in the tiger skin cherished by yogins.
His mind is not on the divine world, nor on a divine bed, nor among crowds;
in the cremation ground, in utmost seclusion, he meditates on me day and night.
From Brahmā down to a blade of grass, Śiva considers [all] equal;
his mind is absorbed in this indescribable form of mine.
Even with the fall of a Brahmā, for the trident-bearer it is but a moment;
as for the measure of his lifespan, I do not know it, let alone the Vedas.
Knowledge, Mṛtyuṃjaya, and the trident possess splendour equal to mine;
except for me, no one is able to conquer that Śaṅkara.

4.38.m:
Connected with Prakṛti, the lord of Prakṛti, the source of nature, and beyond Prakṛti.
You assume various forms for the sake of the meditation of your devotees;
Whatever affection there is for whichever forms, those very forms you assume.

4.40.m:
Prompted by Brahmā, Dakṣa gave his daughter to the trident-bearer;
that reviler of Śiva did not obtain supreme fortune, [he met with] misfortune.
Giving his daughter reluctantly, he obtained a fourth of the merit;
alas, abandoning conformity of form, he obtained petty heaven.
Let someone among you, O gods, go to the mountain's house
and with effort bring about his own design regarding the king of mountains.

Giving his daughter reluctantly, let him remain comfortably in Bhārata;
for if he gives his daughter to him with devotion, he will surely attain liberation.
Afterwards, all the Seven Ṛṣis, taking along Arundhatī,
will surely go to his house and persuade the mountain.
Except for the wielder of the Pināka, Durgā will choose no other groom;
reluctantly he will bestow his daughter upon him at his daughter's command.

4.41.m:
Then he went to Vaikuṇṭha together with Śatarūpā;
having attained servitude and proximity, he became a servant of Hari.
After him, there was Manu Svārociṣa himself, the great one.
When Svārociṣa had passed away, O Mountain, there was Manu Uttama.
When Uttama had departed, the righteous Tāmasa was the Manu;
then Raivata, the best of the wise, became the Manu here.
Next is to be known Cākṣuṣa, and Śrāddhadeva is the seventh;
the eighth is to be known as Sāvarṇi, the great son of the blessed Sūrya.
Formerly on earth there was King Suratha, born in the lineage of Caitra;
the ninth was Dakṣasāvarṇi, and Brahmasāvarṇi the tenth;

the eleventh, the best of Manus, is called Dharmasāvarṇi;
then Rudrasāvarṇi, devoted to Viṣṇu, with senses conquered;
after him Devasāvarṇi, and then Indrasāvarṇi.
Thus the fourteen Manus have been recounted, O friend.
When these have elapsed, one day of Brahmā has occurred.
Hear from me the entire story of Indrasāvarṇi.
Foremost among the Manus, righteous, a pure devotee of the wielder of the mace,
he ruled the kingdom righteously for seventy-one yugas.
Having given the kingdom to Surendra, he went to the forest for austerities.
Surendra’s son was the glorious Śrīniketu of mighty strength;
his son was the great yogin Purīṣataru;
his son was exceedingly resplendent, remembered as Gokāmukha;
Vṛddhaśravas was his son, and his son was Bhānu;
Puṇḍarīka was his son, and likewise his son was Jihvala;
Jihvala’s son was Śṛṅgin, and his son was Bhīma;
his son was Yaśaścandra, by whose fame the moon was surpassed.

The good and the gods constantly sing his spotless fame.
His son was Vareṇya, and Purāraṇya was his son;
his son was the righteous and glorious Dharāraṇya;
his son was Maṅgalāraṇya, an ascetic and best of the wise.

worshipped the lord of Goloka and went indeed to Goloka.
The eldest son of the king became a renowned monarch;
he protected all the subjects on the surface of the earth like his own children.
